-- The cost guide shipped. This row specified it, and building it proved two
-- of this row's own numbers unreproducible -- $1.05/W for a basket that adds
-- to $1.08/W, and ~2.7% for a figure that derives to ~3.6%. Both are recorded
-- above rather than quietly corrected, because a page about prices drifting
-- is the worst possible place to carry a total that has drifted from its own
-- line items, and the fix (compute it in src/lib/solar-cost.ts, test it) is
-- the reusable part.
--
-- in_test rather than in_production: nothing on this page is publishable until
-- the production deploy, the LLC and outside legal review land, and the
-- Section 232 figures carry a 15 September verification that the row itself
-- says to re-date at publish time. The freshness alarm now enforces that
-- rather than trusting anyone to remember it.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

update public.roadmap_items
  set status = 'in_test',
      dev_percent_complete = 85,
      description = 'Differentiator #1 applied to the question every beginner asks first and that the site cannot currently answer at all: as of 15 September 2026 nothing under src/ mentions price per watt, tariffs, tax credits or payback. Seeds differentiator #3 as well -- a $/W benchmark is the first aggregate the community layer can carry ("systems like yours came in at X/W"), which is why this is built as a reference page with a table rather than prose. WHAT IT CARRIES. (1) THE INSTALLED-PRICE GAP. The US pays roughly three times Australia''s installed price for identical hardware, and the cause is not the hardware. Customer acquisition alone ran 23% of a residential system''s price at $0.75/W in H1 2021, more than twice what the module cost that year; marketing, profit and labour together are around 43% of an American system, while modules are about 12% of what a homeowner pays. (2) A REAL BILL OF MATERIALS, so the number is not abstract: 20 x 450 W panels at $130, five 4-in-1 micro-inverters at $330, $2,000 of racking, wire and hardware, $500 of permits and $3,000 of labour -- a 9 kW build at roughly $1.05/W against about $3/W installed. (3) THE IMPORT PRICE FLOOR, and the finding that makes this page ours rather than a rewrite of everyone else''s tariff post. The Section 232 proclamation of 6 August 2026 sets a minimum import price of $0.38/W on modules and $0.22/W on cells, plus a 15% ad valorem duty, effective 12:01 a.m. ET on 4 December 2026. Against a median transacted price near $0.271/W that is about 40% on the module. On a full installed system it is roughly 2.7%, which is why the trade press treats it as a rounding error -- but this site''s reader buys bare pallets, where there is no $28,000 denominator to dilute it. The one audience for whom the floor is a real number is the one nobody is writing for. (4) THE 30% CREDIT IS GONE. P.L. 119-21 section 70506 terminated 26 U.S.C. 25D for expenditures made after 31 December 2025, and 25D already treats an expenditure as made when the original installation is completed -- so paying in 2025 for a 2026 install earns nothing. No cost figure anywhere on this site may assume 30% off. COST STAYS IN GUIDES: the non-goal added to CLAUDE.md alongside this item rules out a payback or $/W calculator, for the Winter-versus-Jeppesen reason the legal posture already sets out. VERIFY BEFORE PUBLISH: the Section 232 figures were confirmed 15 September 2026 against pv magazine USA and Anza. Trade remedies get amended -- re-check and re-date every number on this page at publish time. SCOPED 2026-09-16, from the objection that decided the shape of this page: prices move week to week under tariffs and freight, so what is written today is wrong next month. THE ANSWER IS THAT THE VOLATILITY IS NOT UNIFORM, and a page that mixes the layers decays at the rate of its fastest number. Three layers, three mechanisms. STRUCTURAL claims decay in years or not at all -- the 3x US-versus-Australia installed-price gap, soft costs at ~43% against modules at ~12%, and the reason a tariff that is a rounding error on an installed system is a real number for someone buying bare pallets. That is the half that makes this page ours and it needs no mechanism. CITED claims decay when their source changes: the Section 232 floor and the 25D termination are statutes, not market prices, and they carry a citation, a checked-on date and a published effective date. LIVE prices decay in days and MAY NOT BE TYPED INTO PROSE AT ALL -- they render from a dated source through src/lib/battery-price.ts, which already expires one at 45 days. WHAT THIS CHANGES ABOUT THE PAGE: the worked bill of materials is an illustration carrying the date it was priced, never the claim. The claim is that DIY roughly thirds the installed price because it deletes the soft-cost lines, and that survives a 20% move in module prices. PREREQUISITE, BUILT FIRST: the dated-claim machinery -- src/lib/content-freshness.ts, the registry in guide-claims.ts, the ClaimStamp component and content-freshness.yml. It applies to all twelve existing guides, not just this one, and writing this page without it would add the site''s most perishable prose to the pile nothing was tracking. SEQUENCING CONSEQUENCE: the live module $/W figure cannot ship in v1 because there is no panel_models table -- migrations hold only battery_models, and PANEL_PRESETS has one admitted panel. v1 is the structural layer plus stamped statute claims; the live $/W slot arrives with the panel pipeline. That is a smaller page than this row originally described, and it is the half that will not be wrong next month. SHIPPED 2026-09-16 as /guides/what-solar-costs, built exactly to the scope above: the structural layer plus stamped statute claims, no live module $/W. WHAT IT CARRIES: where the money goes in an installed US system (23% customer acquisition, ~43% marketing-profit-labour, ~12% modules); a worked 9 kW basket; the Section 232 floor and its two denominators; and the 25D termination with the completion-date trap. FIVE CLAIMS REGISTERED in guide-claims.ts, all checked 2026-09-15 rather than the build date, and the Section 232 row carries changesOn 2026-12-04 so its review comes due on the day the floor lands however recently anyone re-read it. That claim is now the nearest-due item on the whole site. TWO ARITHMETIC CORRECTIONS THIS ROW WAS WRONG ABOUT, both caught by computing the totals in src/lib/solar-cost.ts instead of typing them. (1) The basket adds to $1.08/W, not the $1.05/W stated above -- 20x$130 + 5x$330 + $2,000 + $500 + $3,000 = $9,750 over 9,000 W. (2) The floor against a full installed system derives to ~3.6%, not the ~2.7% stated above: ($0.38 - $0.271) / $3.00. Neither figure was reproducible from this row''s own numbers, which is the argument for the module: a page about prices drifting must not carry a total that has drifted from its own line items. AND A THIRD SPLIT THE ROW DID NOT HAVE: doing the work yourself deletes LABOUR, not PERMITS, so the page prints a three-rung ladder -- $0.69/W parts, $0.75/W parts and permits, $1.08/W with paid labour -- against $3.00/W installed. A two-way hardware/soft split would have implied a DIY build skips inspection, which is both false and the last thing this site should imply. NOT COMBINED, DELIBERATELY: the page refuses to state what the price floor and the 15% ad valorem duty come to together, because how they stack depends on entered value and no ruling was read. REMAINING: the live module $/W block still waits on panel_models; the figures still carry their 15 September verification and the page is not publishable until the production and legal gates clear anyway, which is the right order.'
  where title = 'Guide: what a solar system should cost';
